  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nThe subtropical ridge is being pushed eastward thanks to a closed\nlow over the Columbia River Valley. We are on the western side of\nthe ridge with deep southerly flow. Visible satellite shows a \ncluster of clouds over Chihuahua, extending northward into New \nMexico. Given the increasingly agitated state of the cu and alto \ncu, I'd say the clouds are evidence of a weak system or MCV. The \nHRRR is blind to this system, but given a few echos on radar, I'd \nsay it's going to be more of a player in our weather this \nafternoon and evening then what the HRRR shows. Regardless, with \nplenty of moisture in place, we'll see at least scattered coverage\nin the mountains and isolated to widely scattered coverage in the\nlowlands. Highs today will be near record levels, but it will \nalso be the hottest day of the week. \n\nMonday and Tuesday will be more or less a carbon copy of today but\nwith lower temperatures and a bit greater storm coverage. It \nshould be noted the GFS is bullish for Tuesday night into \nWednesday in terms of QPF. We will remain in southerly flow but \nwith increasing influence from the trough/low expected to remain \nquasi-stationary over the Columbia River Valley. This southerly \nflow may also allow more weak waves/disturbances to traverse the \narea. Thus, we'll continue with rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms. PW will be on the increase as well, reaching the \n1.4-1.5\" range, meaning flash flooding will be a concern with any \ncells that train or stall. \n\nThe Wednesday through Friday period looks to be the most active\ntimeframe of the forecast window although details may shift as we\napproach then. Our weather will be influenced by a slow-moving\ntrough. It will lift out of Mexico on Wednesday, reach the Four\nCorners by Thursday, and then deepen over NM on Friday with its\ntrough axis somewhere near the Rio Grande Valley. The system\nfinally pulls out of the area on Saturday with drier air behind\nit. We will see multiple rounds of rain with the slow-moving\ntrough with heavy rain and flash flooding the main concern.\nTemperatures will also be cooler and closer to normal for mid-\nSeptember. With the trough's departure on Saturday, we will see\nthat drier air but rain / storm coverage will continue with much\nless coverage.",
